  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "The Maxwell relations are extraordinarily useful in deriving the dependence",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "maxwell-relations",
   "prompt": "<p>A van der Waals gas obeys $\\kp = \\dfrac{\\knmol\\kR\\kT}{\\kV - \\knmol b} - \\dfrac{a\\knmol^2}{\\kV^2}$. Use the Maxwell relation from $\\kA$ to find $\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial \\kSent}{\\partial \\kV}\\right)_{\\kT}$ for 1.00 mol of $\\mathrm{N_2}$ ($b = 0.03913$ L/mol) in a volume of 1.00 L.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 8.65,
    "unit": "J/(K·L)",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "By Table 22.3.1, $(\\partial \\kSent/\\partial \\kV)_{\\kT} = (\\partial \\kp/\\partial \\kT)_{\\kV}$, and only the first term of $\\kp$ depends on $\\kT$.",
    "solution": "<p>The Maxwell relation from $\\kA$ is $(\\partial \\kSent/\\partial \\kV)_{\\kT} = (\\partial \\kp/\\partial \\kT)_{\\kV}$. At constant $\\kV$ the attraction term $a\\knmol^2/\\kV^2$ does not change with $\\kT$, so $(\\partial \\kp/\\partial \\kT)_{\\kV} = \\knmol\\kR/(\\kV - \\knmol b)$. With the numbers, $(1.00)(8.314\\ \\text{J K}^{-1})/(1.00\\ \\text{L} - 0.0391\\ \\text{L}) = 8.65$ J/(K·L), a little more than the ideal gas's $\\knmol\\kR/\\kV = 8.31$ J/(K·L), because the molecules' own volume leaves less room to expand into.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
